WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.980
A worked example is more than a finished answer.

2
00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.046
It makes the steps and reasons visible.

3
00:00:06.246 --> 00:00:12.356
Build a complete model, then a partially supported example, then a fresh independent task.

4
00:00:12.556 --> 00:00:19.466
For the plant inquiry, demonstrate how one observation supports a location choice and name the uncertainty.

5
00:00:19.666 --> 00:00:25.077
Say why you use that observation rather than simply describing which object you clicked.

6
00:00:25.277 --> 00:00:30.897
Duplicate the model’s useful structure and remove one step that learners are ready to supply.

7
00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:35.588
You might keep the claim and observations but remove the explanation.

8
00:00:35.788 --> 00:00:39.335
Ask a partner to complete it and justify the choice.

9
00:00:39.535 --> 00:00:42.936
Avoid removing every support at once for a novice.

10
00:00:43.136 --> 00:00:49.824
Listen for the misconception before offering the answer; the learner’s explanation tells you which part needs

11
00:00:50.024 --> 00:00:51.228
another model.

12
00:00:51.428 --> 00:00:56.210
The final task should change the example while preserving the reasoning demand.

13
00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:01.730
A student who can copy the original answer has not necessarily transferred the idea.

14
00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:05.475
Give a new observation and ask for a new explanation.

15
00:01:05.675 --> 00:01:12.658
Use one individual response to decide whether to move ahead, provide a smaller scaffold or reteach a connection.

16
00:01:12.858 --> 00:01:18.697
Pause and identify the step you will model, the step you will fade and the new case you will use.
